By Du Fu

The wind is sharp, the sky is high; gibbons wail in sorrow.
The islet is clear, the sand is white; birds circle back.
Boundless, the falling leaves come rustling down;
Endless, the Yangtze River comes rolling on.
Ten thousand miles away, I grieve at autumn—always a traveler;
A hundred years of sickness, I climb this terrace alone.
Hardship and bitter regret have thickened the frost on my temples;
Worn out, I have lately stopped my cup of cloudy wine.

