By Du Fu

The wind is sharp, the sky is high — the gibbons wail their grief;
The isle is clear, the sand is white — the birds wheel back in flight.
A boundless fall of leaves comes rustling, rustling down;
The endless Yangtze surges, surges on.

Ten thousand miles from home, I grieve at autumn, forever a sojourner;\nA hundred years of ailments — alone I climb this terrace.
Hardship and bitter regret have thickened my frost-white temples;
In my down-and-out decline, I have only just set down my cup of turbid wine."}

translation:
The wind is sharp, the sky is high, gibbons cry in sorrow;
The islet is clear, the sand is white, birds wheel in flight.
Boundless, the falling leaves come rustling down;
Endless, the Yangtze River rolls on toward me.
Ten thousand miles from home, grieving at autumn, forever a traveler;
All my life, often ill, I climb this terrace alone.
Hardship and bitter regret have thickened the frost on my temples;
Down and out, I have lately stopped my cup of turbid wine.

