Where maple leaves and reed flowers rustled in autumn's chill light.
The host dismounted, the guest stepped aboard his boat;
We raised our cups to drink, but no lute or flute played note.
Drunk, yet joyless, we neared the moment of parting;
The river drowned in mist, the moonlight slowly setting.
Suddenly from the water came a pipa's sound;
The host forgot to leave, the guest his boat unbound.
We sought the source and softly asked, "Who plays?"—
The music stopped; the player hesitated, half in fear.
We moved our boat near hers, invited her to appear;
More wine was brought, lamps lit, the feast began again.
After a thousand calls, at last she came out,
Still hugging her pipa, half her face in shade about.
She tuned the pegs, plucked the strings two, three times—
A feeling born before the melody's first chimes.
Each string, each note, was choked with deep emotion;
She seemed to tell of lifelong thwarted devotion.
With lowered brows, she idly played on, string by string,
Telling all the countless things her heart did bring.
Light pressing, slow twisting, stroking, then a flick—
First the Rainbow Skirt, then the Six Minor quick.
The low strings moaned like sudden rain;
The high strings whispered like secrets in pain.
The two entwined, a tumble, a clash, a blend—
Like great and small pearls falling on a jade dish-end.
Like orioles singing softly under flowers in flight;
Like a spring choked with ice, its flow blocked from light.
The cold ice stiffened, the strings' pulse nearly dead;\nSilence fell, the sound no longer spread.
Yet from that silence, hidden sorrows rose,
That wordless pause spoke more than any sound that flows.
Then—like a silver vase breaking, water bursting out;
Like armored cavalry charging, swords and spears about.
At the song's end, she swept the拨 across the strings with might—
Four strings rang as one, like silk torn clean in the night.
East boats, west barges, all sat hushed, no word;
Only the autumn moon on the river's heart was seen, unstirred.
She tucked the拨 among the strings, sank in thought;\nAdjusted her clothes, composed the face she'd taught.
"I was a girl of the capital, you see,
My home stood beneath Hama Hill's estate.
At thirteen I mastered the pipa's art,
Ranked first among the Music Bureau's part.
When I finished a song, even masters bowed;
When I dressed up, even beauties envied, and how!
The young blades of Wuling fought to shower me with gifts;
One song brought red silk beyond counting, in drifts.
Jeweled combs snapped keeping time, in fragments, in bits;
Blood-red skirts were stained by wine in its fits.
Year after year of laughter, of endless play;
Autumn moons, spring breeys, all wasted away.
My brother left for war, my aunt died;
Dawn came, dusk went, my youth slipped by.
Fewer horses stopped before my door;
I grew old, and married a merchant, poor.
The merchant values profit, not love, not staying;
Last month he sailed to Fuliang, his tea trade displaying.
I wait alone by the river mouth in my empty boat;
Around me, the moon shines bright, the water cold and remote.
Deep in the night, I dream of my youthful years;
I wake weeping, my tears cutting red rivers through my fears."
I had sighed already at the pipa's song;
Hearing her words, I sighed again, all along.
Both of us are wanderers at the world's end;
Why must we have met before to call each other friend?
Last year I left the capital's bright scene;\nExiled, ill, I dwell in Xunyang, sick and mean.
Xunyang is far from music, far from cheer;
All year I hear no lute or flute, year after year.
I live near the Pen River, low and damp;
Yellow reeds and bitter bamboo crowd my camp.
What do I hear from morn to night?
Cuckoos crying blood, gibbons' mournful plight.
On flower-filled spring days, autumn moonlit nights,
I often take my wine and drink alone, in spite.
Are there no mountain songs or village flutes?
They're harsh and grating—no charm, no salutes.
But tonight, hearing your pipa's speech,
Feels like immortal music, bright and fresh.
Please don't refuse—sit, play one more song;
For you, I'll turn this into a pipa song, strong.
Moved by my words, she stood long in place;
Then sat back, tightened strings, quickened the pace.
The melody, mournful, unlike the sound before;
The whole room wept to hear it once more.
Among the guests, who wept the most?
The Jiangzhou Marshal—his blue gown was wet, lost."}

