formatted_result:
{"translation": "At Xunyang River's head I saw a guest off by night;
Maple leaves and reed flowers shiver in the autumn chill.
The host dismounts, the guest boards his boat;
We raise our cups to drink, but there is no music.
Half drunk but not in spirits, we part in gloom;
The parting scene: a vast expanse of river soaked in moonlight.
Suddenly from the water comes the sound of a pipa;
The host forgets to go home, the guest delays his departure.
Following the sound, I ask softly who is playing;
The pipa stops, and she hesitates to answer.
We move our boat near and invite her out;
Pour more wine, relight the lamp, and set the feast anew.
After a thousand calls, at last she emerges,
Still holding the pipa, half hiding her face.
She turns the pegs, plucks the strings two or three times;
Before a tune is formed, feeling already stirs.
Each string, each note, suppressed yet full of thought,
As if recounting a life of unfulfilled ambition.
Brows lowered, she plays on at her ease,
Telling all the endless things within her heart.
Lightly gathering, slowly twisting, rubbing and picking,
First the Rainbow Skirt Dance, then the Six Beats.
The great strings roar like sudden rain,
The small strings whisper like private speech.
Roars and whispers interlaced in playing,
Like pearls, great and small, dropping on a jade plate.
Like orioles warbling under flowers, the notes glide smooth;
Like a hidden spring choking under ice, they strain.
Ice-cold water, the strings grow stiff and snap;
The sound, blocked, halts for a while.
A separate sorrow, a secret grief arises;
At this moment, silence surpasses sound.
A silver vase suddenly shatters, water gushing forth;\Iron cavalry bursts out, swords and spears clashing.
The tune ends, the plectrum sweeps across the strings;
Four strings at once, like tearing silk.
Boats east and west, all silent, no one speaks;
Only the autumn moon, white, in the river's heart.
Pondering, she tucks the plectrum among the strings,
Straightens her garments, rises, composes her face.
She says: "I was once a girl of the capital,
My home was by Hama Hill.
At thirteen I mastered the pipa,
My name was listed first in the Music Bureau.
After playing, even the master musicians admired me;
When I dressed up, the beauties envied me.
The young gallants of Wuling competed to shower me with gifts;
For one song, I received countless red silks.
My silver hairpin beat time till it snapped;
My blood-red silk skirt was stained with spilled wine.
Year after year of laughter and song,
The autumn moon and spring breeze passed by at ease.
My younger brother went to war, my aunt passed away;
Night became morning, and my looks grew old.
Before my gate, carriages grew few, horses scarce;
Growing old, I married a merchant.
He values profit and thinks little of parting;
Last month he went to Fuliang to buy tea.
He comes and goes; I guard this empty boat at the river's mouth,
Around the boat, the bright moon, the river water cold.
Deep in the night, I suddenly dream of my youth;
In my dream I weep, my makeup streaked with tears." 
I had sighed at the first pipa song;
Hearing these words, I sigh again and again.
We both are wanderers at the edge of the world;
Why must we have known each other before to meet like this?
Last year I took leave of the imperial capital,
Demoted and ill, I dwell in Xunyang.
Xunyang is remote, with no music;
Year round, I hear no string or flute.
I live near the Pen River, low and damp;
Yellow reeds and bitter bamboo grow around my house.
What do I hear here, morning and evening?
The cuckoo crying blood, the gibbon's mournful wail.
On flower-filled spring mornings, moonlit autumn nights,
I often pour my wine and drink alone.
Are there no hill songs or village flutes?
Their crude, discordant notes are hard to listen to.
Tonight I hear your pipa's speech;
Like hearing immortal music, my ears are briefly cleared.
Do not refuse; sit and play one more song,
For you, I will turn it into this Pipa Ballad.
Moved by my words, she stands for a long time;
Then sits back, tightens the strings, and quickens the tempo.
The sad, sad notes are not like the sounds before;
The whole company, hearing it again, weeps.
Who among us shed the most tears?
The Prefect of Jiangzhou, his blue robe all wet."}

