translation:
Seeing a Guest Off at Xunyang River

At Xunyang River's head I saw a guest off by night;
The maple leaves and reed flowers shivered in autumn's chill.
I dismounted; my guest boarded his boat.
We raised our cups, about to drink, but there was no music.
Drunk without joy, we grieved at our impending parting;
At the moment of farewell, the vast river was flooded with moonlight.
Suddenly we heard a pipa's sound upon the water;
The host forgot to go home, the guest delayed his departure.
Following the sound, I asked softly who was playing;
The pipa stopped, and she hesitated to answer.
We moved our boat nearer and invited her to meet us;
We added wine, relit the lamps, and reopened the feast.
After a thousand calls and ten thousand entreaties she finally emerged,
Still cradling her pipa, half hiding her face.
She turned the pegs and plucked the strings a few times;
Before the melody was formed, feeling was already there.
Every string, every phrase was suppressed and brooding, full of thought,
As if telling of a life of unfulfilled ambition.
Brows lowered, she played on carelessly and fluently,
Telling all the countless things within her heart.
Lightly gathering, slowly twisting, stroking and plucking,
First she played "Rainbow Skirts," then "Little Branch."
On the thick strings, a clamor like sudden rain;
On the thin strings, a whisper like private talk.
The clamor and whisper interwove and crossed,
Like great pearls and small pearls falling on a jade plate.
Then, like orioles warbling smoothly beneath the flowers;
Then, like a hidden stream choking under ice.
The icy spring grew cold and harsh; the strings congealed and broke;
The sound froze, could not pass on, and paused for a time.
A separate sorrow, a hidden resentment rose:
At this moment, silence was better than sound.
Then, like a silver vase suddenly shattering, water bursting forth;
Like armored cavalry charging out, swords and spears clashing.
At the song's end, she drew the pick across the center of the strings;
The four strings at once sounded like tearing silk.
In boats east and west, all was silent;
Only the autumn moon, white, was seen in the river's heart.
Muttering to herself, she tucked the pick among the strings;
Straightened her clothes, rose, and composed her countenance.
She said: "I was once a girl of the capital,
My home was beneath Hama Hill.
At thirteen I mastered the pipa,
My name was listed first in the Music Bureau.
When I finished a song, even the best musicians admired me;
When I dressed up, even Qiu Niang was jealous of me.
The young men of Wuling competed to shower me with gifts;
For one song, I received countless bolts of red silk.
Ornamented combs and silver hairpins were smashed keeping time;
Blood-red silk skirts were stained and spoiled by spilled wine.
Year after year of laughter and joy,
The autumn moons and spring winds passed by without notice.
My younger brother left for the army; my aunt died;
Dawns and dusks went by, and my youth faded.
Before my door, carriages and horses grew few and cold;
Growing old, I married a merchant.
Merchants value profit and lightly regard separation;
Last month he went to Fuliang to buy tea.
He comes and goes; I wait by the river mouth in an empty boat;
The moon shines bright around the boat, and the river water is cold.
Late at night, I suddenly dream of my youth;
In my dream I weep, and my makeup runs in red streaks down my face."
I had sighed upon hearing her pipa;
Hearing her words, I sighed again, again.
We are both people fallen to the ends of the earth;
Why must we have known each other before to meet?"
Since last year I took leave of the imperial capital,
I have lived in illness, exiled in Xunyang city.
Xunyang is remote and has no music;
All year I have not heard the sound of strings and flutes.
I live near the Pen River, a low and damp place;
Yellow reeds and bitter bamboo grow thick around my house.
What do I hear here, morning and evening?
Cuckoos crying blood, gibbons howling their grief.
On flower-viewing spring days and moonlit autumn nights,
I often take my wine and drink alone.
Are there no mountain songs or village flutes?
Their squawking and discordant noise is hard to bear.
Tonight, hearing your pipa's language,
It is like hearing immortal music; my ears are briefly enlightened.
Do not refuse to sit and play one more song;
For you, I will compose this "Song of the Pipa."
Moved by my words, she stood for a long time;
Then she sat back, tightened the strings, and the tempo quickened.
The mournful notes were not like the earlier sound;
The whole assembly, hearing it again, covered their faces and wept.
Who among the assembly wept the most?
The Jiangzhou Vice-Prefect, his blue robe soaked with tears."

