translation:
A Song of the Pipa

By Bai Juyi

At Xunyang River's head, I saw a friend off on an autumn night,
The maple leaves and reed flowers rustled in the chill.
The host dismounted, the guest boarded his boat;\nWe raised our cups to drink, but there was no string or pipe music.
Drunk, yet not merry, we were about to part in sorrow;
At parting, the vast river was flooded with moonlight.
Suddenly, from the water came the sound of a pipa;
The host forgot to return, the guest delayed his departure.
Seeking the sound, we softly asked who was playing;
The pipa stopped, and she hesitated to speak.
We moved our boat nearer and invited her to meet;
Added wine, relit the lamps, and reopened the banquet.
After a thousand calls and ten thousand entreaties, she finally came out,
Still holding the pipa, half hiding her face.
She turned the pegs and plucked the strings a few times;
Before the melody formed, the emotion was already there.
Each string, each note, was choked with feeling, each sound full of thought;
As if telling of a life of unfulfilled ambition.
With lowered brows, casually and continuously she played;
Telling all the endless matters in her heart.
Lightly gathering, slowly twisting, stroking and plucking;
First she played 'Rainbow Skirt and Feathered Coat,' then 'Six Yao.'
The large strings roared like a sudden rainstorm;
The small strings whispered like private conversation.
The roaring and whispering were played in a complex mix;
Like large and small pearls falling on a jade plate.
Then, like an oriole's warble, smooth over flowers;
Then like a muffled spring, struggling under ice.
The icy spring grew cold and astringent, the strings congealed and broke;
The sound, congealed and blocked, paused for a time.
A separate, hidden sorrow and secret resentment were born;
At this moment, the silence was more eloquent than sound.
Then, like a silver vase suddenly shattering, water and liquor bursting forth;
Like an iron cavalry charging out, swords and spears clashing.
At the song's end, she swept the拨 (plectrum) across the center;
The four strings at once, like tearing silk.
The boats east and west were silent;
Only the autumn moon in the river's heart was white.
Thoughtfully, she put the拨 among the strings;
Adjusted her clothes, stood up, and composed her countenance.
She said, 'I was originally a woman of the capital;
My home was below Hama Ling.
At thirteen, I mastered the pipa;
My name was listed first in the Teaching Bureau.
When I finished a song, even skilled musicians were impressed;
When my makeup was done, even beauties like Qiu Niang were jealous.
The young men of Wuling competed to offer gifts;
For one song, I received countless red silks.
Gold and silver hairpins were broken to keep time;
Blood-red silk skirts were stained and spoiled by spilled wine.
One year of joy followed another;
The autumn moon and spring breeze passed by idly.
My younger brother went to the army, my aunt died;
Day and night passed, and my youthful looks faded.
In front of my door, carriages and horses grew sparse;
Growing old, I married a merchant.
The merchant valued profit and lightly regarded parting;
Last month, he went to Fuliang to buy tea.
Coming and going, I guard an empty boat at the river's mouth;
Around the boat, the moon is bright, the river water cold.
In the deep night, I suddenly dreamed of my youth;
In the dream, I cried, and my tears streaked my red makeup.'
I had sighed upon hearing the pipa;
Hearing her words, I sighed again, again.
We are both people fallen to the ends of the earth;
Why must we have known each other before to meet?
Since last year, I resigned from the capital;
Demoted and ill, I lie in Xunyang city.
Xunyang is remote and has no music;
All year round, I do not hear the sound of strings and flutes.
Living near the Pan River, the land is low and damp;
Yellow reeds and bitter bamboo grow around my house.
What do I hear here morning and evening?
The cuckoo's bloody cry and the gibbon's mournful wail.
On spring river flower-mornings and autumn moon-nights;
Often, I take my wine and drink alone.
Are there no mountain songs and village flutes?
Their crude, jarring sound is hard to listen to.
Tonight, hearing your pipa's language;
Is like hearing celestial music, my ears temporarily enlightened.
Do not refuse to sit and play one more song;
For you, I will compose a 'Song of the Pipa.'
Moved by my words, she stood for a long time;
Then sat back and quickened the strings, the tempo grew fast.
The mournful sound was not like the previous melodies;
The whole audience, hearing it again, wept.
Who in the seats shed the most tears?
The Jiangzhou Sima, his blue robe wet.

