The wind is swift, the sky high, apes wail mournfully; 
Islets clear, sands white, birds wheel and return. 
Endless falling leaves rustle in the chill; 
The endless Yangtze rolls on without rest. 
A thousand miles of sorrow in autumn, I wander as a guest; 
In my hundred years of illness, alone I climb this platform. 
Hardship and bitterness have silvered my temples with frost; 
Now destitute, I've just stopped drinking my turbid wine.

