The wind is sharp, the sky is high, the gibbons cry with sorrow;
The islet clear, the sand white, birds wheel in flight.
Boundless, the falling leaves come rustling down;
Endless, the Yangtze rolls on, wave after wave.
Ten thousand miles of autumn grief, forever a stranger;\nA hundred years of many-layered sickness, alone I climb this terrace.
Hardship and bitter regret thicken the frost on my temples;
Down and out, I have lately stopped my cup of turbid wine.

