The nation is shattered, yet hills and streams remain;
The city in spring, where grass and trees grow wild.
Moved by the times, flowers splash with tears;
Hating separation, birds startle the heart.
Beacon fires stretch through three long months;
A letter from home is worth ten thousand in gold.
I scratch my white hair, growing thinner still—
Soon it will be too scant to hold a hairpin.

