message.content:
The wind is sharp, the sky so high; the gibbons cry.
On isles, clear streams, white sands, birds circle in the air.
Boundless, the falling leaves come rustling down below;
Endless, the Yangtze rolls its mighty waves and flows.
Ten thousand miles away, in autumn's grief I roam;
A hundred years of ills, I climb the terrace all alone.
Life’s hardships and my hate have frosted o'er my hair;
Down and out, I’ve just now stopped my cup of turbid wine.

The wind is high, the sky is vast; the gibbons cry with grief,
The islet clear, the sand snow-white; birds wheel and turn.
Boundless, the withered leaves come rustling down;
Endless, the great Yangtze comes surging up.
Ten thousand li, a traveler in sad autumn;
A hundred years, sick and alone, I climb this terrace.
Hardship and bitter sorrow have thickened my frosty temples;
In wretchedness, I have lately stopped my cup of turbid wine.

