message.content:
The wind is gone, the dust smells sweet, the flowers all are spent;
The day grows late, too weary now to comb my hair.
The world remains, the people gone, all things to naught are bent;
I’d speak, but tears come first, and fall before my prayer.

I hear the Twin Streams still hold their spring, and fair it is;
I thought to drift in a light boat, and let the season stir.
But I fear those little boats upon the Twin Streams,
Could never bear the weight of so much sorrow in them.

