message.content:
The wind blows sharp, the sky so high, sad gibbons cry;
On isles of white sand, birds circle in the clear sky.
Boundless, the falling leaves descend with rustling sound;
Endless, the Yangtze rolls and surges all around.

Ten thousand miles away, I grieve that autumn’s here;
A lifetime sick, I climb the terrace, all alone.
My troubled life has bleached my hair with frost and fear;
Now, down and out, I’ve even put my wine cup down.

**Ascending the Heights**

The wind is sharp, the sky is vast, the gibbons’ cries are bleak,
The islet’s clear, the sand is white, the birds wheel round and back.
Boundless, the falling leaves come rustling, rustling down;
Endless, the Yangtze’s waters roll, roll on their track.

Ten thousand miles, I grieve at autumn, ever far from home;
One hundred years, oft ill, I climb alone this height.
Hardship and hate have thickened the frost upon my temples;
Even now, I’ve just laid down my cup of turbid wine.

