translation:
Climbing High
By Du Fu

The wind is sharp, the sky so high, the gibbons' cries are mournful;
The islets clear, the sand white, birds wheel in flight.
Boundless, the falling leaves rustle down;
Endless, the Yangtze rolls and surges on.
Ten thousand miles, in autumn grief a perpetual stranger;\nA hundred years, with many ailments, alone I climb this terrace.
Hardship and bitterness have thickened my frosty temples;
Worn out, I've lately stopped my cup of turbid wine.

