translation:
The State is shattered, but hills and streams remain;
In spring the city’s grass and trees grow rank.
Moved by the season, flowers splash with tears;
Hating parting, birds startle the heart.
Beacon fires have burned for three straight months;
A family letter is worth ten thousand gold.
I scratch my white hair, grown ever thinner—
Too scant, at last, to hold the hairpin.

