translation:
Climbing High  
The wind is swift, the sky vast—apes wail mournfully;  
On islets clear, sands white, birds wheel and return.  
Endless falling leaves rustle down in sorrow;  
Unending, the Yangtze rolls on in ceaseless flow.  
Ten thousand miles of grief in autumn, ever a guest;  
A hundred years of illness, alone I climb this height.  
Hardship and bitter regret have whitened my temples with frost;  
In poverty's decline, I've just laid down my muddy wine cup.

