High winds, clear sky—apes wail with mournful cries;  
Islands gleam white, sands bright—birds wheel and return.  
Endless falling leaves rustle down in pairs;  
The endless Yangtze rolls on without pause.  
A thousand miles of sorrow in autumn—I’ve wandered far as a guest;  
A hundred years of illness—alone I climb this platform.  
Hardship, bitterness, and hatred have frosted my temples;  
In poverty and decline, I’ve just stopped drinking my cloudy wine.

